      explanation: 'In a hypertonic solution, solute concentration outside the cell is higher than inside, meaning water concentration outside is lower. Water moves by osmosis from high water concentration (inside the cell) to low water concentration (outside), causing the cell to lose water and shrink.',
      diveDeep: 'Osmosis: water moves passively (no energy required) across a semipermeable membrane from high water potential (low solute) to low water potential (high solute). Hypertonic solution: more solute outside → less water outside → water flows out → cell shrinks (animal cells crenate; plant cells undergo plasmolysis). Hypotonic solution: less solute outside → more water outside → water flows in → cell swells (animal cells may lyse; plant cells develop turgor pressure, become turgid). Isotonic solution: equal solute → no net water movement. Active transport (choices C and D) uses ATP to move solutes against concentration gradients — osmosis of water is always passive. Students should memorize the three scenarios and be able to predict cell behavior in each.',
